If you consider the (sometimes vast) differences in pronunciation in regional variants of the same language, it may be, as you say, an issue with linguistics as a whole.

-----Original Message-----
From: lojban-beginners@googlegroups.com [mailto:lojban-beginners@googlegroups.com]On Behalf Of Luke Bergen
Sent: 30 August 2011 14:45
To: lojban-beginners@googlegroups.com
Subject: Re: [lojban-beginners] Re: Lojban "r"


It sounds like this might be a more generic problem than just "er".  Do you have trouble hearing a different between "ar" and "air" as well? 

Does "Vi" just tend to sound like "V" lengthened?  Is this just a generic issue in linguistics in general?  


On Tue, Aug 30, 2011 at 9:38 AM, Jonathan Jones < eyeonus@gmail.com> wrote:


That doesn't work. All Lojban words are penultimately stressed, excepting specially marked cmene. 


On Tue, Aug 30, 2011 at 7:30 AM, Michael Eaton < michael.eaton@blackpool.gov.uk> wrote:



Personally, the most effective way I can think of to keep, for instance, 'lrfu' and 'lerfu' distinct would be through inflection, rather than pronunciation - thereby ensuring you don't overlap with {eir}.

eg:

Lrfu = lerFU
Lerfu = LERfu

-----Original Message-----
From: lojban-beginners@googlegroups.com [mailto: lojban-beginners@googlegroups.com]On Behalf Of Luke Bergen
Sent: 30 August 2011 14:18
To: lojban-beginners@googlegroups.com
Subject: Re: [lojban-beginners] Re: Lojban "r"


When you said "lrfu" and "lerfu" are the same for you, you're effectively saying that either "lrfu" has an arbitrary "e" inserted OR you're saying that "lerfu" drops the "e".  Either way, it's discomfiting.


On Tue, Aug 30, 2011 at 9:14 AM, Jonathan Jones < eyeonus@gmail.com> wrote:


No. There is no silent e in "better". A silent e is one that makes no sound. There is a silent e in "Euclid", "hate", "bite". 


On Mon, Aug 29, 2011 at 7:07 PM, Viral Lamb < jms1992@comcast.net> wrote:


you pronounce "er" like the "er" in "better"? That means your using a
silent 'e'?

On Aug 29, 6:01 pm, Jonathan Jones < eyeo...@gmail.com> wrote:
> I pronounce {er} like in "bett*er*". I pronounce {eir} like in "*air*". The

> two do not sound alike, and I don't have Luke's problem with thinking it
> sounds too "Englishy" to pronounce {er} like that.
>
>
>
>
>
>
>
>
>

> On Mon, Aug 29, 2011 at 3:35 PM, Viral Lamb < jms1...@comcast.net> wrote:
> > Uh, not being able to tell the difference between the 'e' and 'ei'
> > sound because of the letter 'r' does have the potential for
> > misunderstandings. This isn't just a "oh well its close enough", this
> > goes very much against one of the founding principles of the language,
> > audiovisual isomorphism. Considering all the different cmene, lujvo,
> > and fu'ivla you could create, I see lots of room for this problem to
> > crop up.
>
> > And no, its not just "some people" who have a problem pronouncing "er"
> > and "eir" differently, its everyone from what I can see. I've yet to
> > see how anyone "could" pronounce them differently. If anyone would
> > like to prove me wrong I'm all ears.
